    "decisions": [
      {
        "decision": "Refuted",
        "rationale": "Disproportionate reporting: 11,286 reports, all three predefined rules met (PRR 41.50 (40.58\u201342.43)). The exact term was not found in the label by text matching, but the atorvastatin label already warns of increases in HbA1c and fasting serum glucose (Warnings and Precautions), so the glycaemic risk is labelled; this is a synonym miss, not a new finding. In the 50 most recent reports atorvastatin was a suspect drug in only 24% and concomitant in most, consistent with statins being widely co-prescribed in people with diabetes (confounding by co-medication and co-morbidity). Reporting is decreasing, 96% of reports are serious and lawyers are among the reporters, which suggests stimulated reporting. No new safety information beyond the labelled risk. Refuted as a new signal; no action beyond routine monitoring.",
