    "decisions": [
      {
        "decision": "Potential signal (further evaluation warranted)",
        "rationale": "Disproportionate reporting: 838 reports, all three predefined rules met, increasing reporting trend; the event is on the EMA Designated Medical Event list. Not found in the US Ozempic label version searched (effective 2026-07-30). In the 50 most recent reports semaglutide was a suspect drug in 100%. No positive dechallenge (16 negative, 34 not reported), but optic nerve ischaemia usually causes permanent damage, so recovery after stopping would not be expected. Limitations: the treated population has diabetes and obesity, themselves risk factors (confounding by indication); 82% of reports are from consumers and reporting may be stimulated by publicity and litigation; age is missing in 38%. External context: the EMA safety committee concluded in 2025 that NAION is a very rare side effect of semaglutide and the EU product information was updated; on 9 November 2026 the FDA required a class-wide GLP-1 label update adding NAION to Warnings and Precautions, so the label searched here predates that change. Further evaluation warranted: review case narratives for ophthalmologist confirmation and time to onset, compare with other GLP-1 receptor agonists, and re-run the label check once the updated US label is published.",
