      {
        "decision": "Refuted",
        "rationale": "Disproportionate reporting: 3,221 reports, all three predefined rules met (statistics frozen in this entry). The term was not found in the atorvastatin label, as expected: raised blood cholesterol is the condition atorvastatin is used to treat, not an adverse reaction. Case review supports confounding by indication: in the 50 most recent reports the reported indication for atorvastatin was blood cholesterol increased itself, and atorvastatin was a suspect drug in only 22%. These reports most likely describe the underlying disease or insufficient lipid lowering (lack of effect) rather than a harm caused by the drug. No plausible mechanism for atorvastatin raising cholesterol. Refuted as an adverse reaction signal; lack-of-effect reports would be handled as a separate efficacy or product-quality question if a pattern were seen.",
